# Git attributes for TCMS — line endings, and the files git must not try to merge.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- line endings ----
#
# Verified safe when added (2026-08-02): no tracked file contained a CR, so this normalizes
# nothing and rewrites no blob. It is here to keep it that way — the product is developed on
# macOS and deployed to Linux, and `deploy/deploy.sh` and `backend/gradlew` are executed on the
# far side of that boundary.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The one file that wants CRLF: a Windows batch script fails on LF line endings.
*.bat text eol=crlf

# ------------------------------------------------------------ never auto-merge ----
#
# `-merge` means: on a conflict, keep OUR version as the tentative result and declare the merge
# conflicted. It does NOT mean "take ours" — the merge stays conflicted and will not commit until
# a person resolves it. That is what these files need: git can produce a plausible-looking blend
# of two sides, and for a generated or stamped file every such blend is wrong.
#
# This is not about parallel work. Two agents in one tree overwrote each other three times on
# 2026-08-02 with no parallel run involved at all.

# GENERATED from docs/specs/openapi.yaml. There is no correct textual merge of two regenerations.
# Resolve the CONTRACT, then regenerate the output from it:
#     (resolve docs/specs/openapi.yaml)
#     cd frontend && npm run gen:api
# `linguist-generated` also collapses it in review, which is right — the contract is the review
# surface and this file is its output.
frontend/src/api/schema.d.ts linguist-generated=true -merge

# GENERATED by npm. Same rule, different tool: resolve package.json, run `npm install`, commit what
# it writes. A hand-merged lockfile is how two machines resolve one dependency range differently.
frontend/package-lock.json linguist-generated=true -merge

# STAMPED, not authored — these carry a `Baseline commit` and a *prepended* session log, so any
# concurrent write collides in both places, and a "resolved" baseline stamp is meaningless because
# the correct value is the merge commit, which does not exist until the resolution is finished.
# Rewrite the file rather than merging it.
docs/agent-context/*.md -merge

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- what is absent ----
#
# Flyway migrations are deliberately NOT listed, because no attribute can help: two branches each
# adding a `V20__*.sql` create two different FILENAMES and git sees no conflict at all. Same for the
# fixture UUID prefix space in the test suite. Both are handled by convention or not at all — noted
# here because "there's a .gitattributes entry for it" is a reasonable assumption and a wrong one.
